<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Beylik of Titteri</h1><p class="st-meta">1548 CE – 1830 CE (approx.) · capital Médéa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The smallest of the three provinces of Ottoman Algiers, governed from Médéa by beys who controlled the highlands south of the capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a> organised its territory in the mid-16th century into the provinces of the East, the West and Titteri, with the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> itself ruled directly. Titteri, created around 1548, covered the Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> and high plains south of Algiers, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medea">Médéa</a> as its seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The bey, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, collected taxes from tribes and farmers, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissary</a> garrisons and allied makhzen tribes. Every few years he travelled to Algiers to deliver tribute in person. The province supplied grain, livestock and wool to the capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medea">Médéa</a> was a market town with Andalusi and Ottoman influences.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the French captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1830, the bey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustapha-boumezrag">Mustapha Boumezrag</a> first submitted and then resisted. French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medea">Médéa</a> in late 1830, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> was dissolved into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/beylik-of-titteri.jpg" alt="Beylik of Titteri" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: AtlasDzMapper · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eyalet_of_Alg%C3%A9ria_in_the_1700.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1548 CE – 1830 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Médéa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mustapha Boumezrag</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber, Ottoman Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Titteri</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1689&amp;focus=beylik-of-titteri" aria-label="Beylik of Titteri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4749 869 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Beylik of Titteri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5069 1125L5077 1108M5069 1125L5077 1108"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5077 1108h0M5077 1108h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5069 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5069 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Médéa. <a href="/atlas?year=1689&amp;focus=beylik-of-titteri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

